{title:Title}

{pstd}{bf:cgeonear} {hline 2} Find nearest neighbors and calculate geodetic
distances

{marker syntax}{...}

{title:Syntax}

{pstd}Nearest neighbors (the form of {cmd:geonear})

{p 8 16 2}{cmd:cgeonear} {it:baseid baselat baselon} {cmd:using} {it:nborfile}{cmd:,}
{opt n:eighbors(nborid nborlat nborlon)} [{it:neighbor_options}]

{pstd}Distances, if one or more coordinates are numeric variables (the form of
{cmd:geodist})

{p 8 16 2}{cmd:cgeonear} {it:lat1 lon1 lat2 lon2} {ifin}{cmd:,}
{opt g:enerate(newvar)} [{it:distance_options}]

{pstd}Distances, if all coordinates are numeric scalars or numbers

{p 8 16 2}{cmd:cgeonear} {it:lat1 lon1 lat2 lon2} [{cmd:,} {it:distance_options}]

{synoptset 22 tabbed}{...}
{synopthdr:neighbor_options}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Main}
{synopt:{opt wid:e}}return nearest neighbors in wide form (the default){p_end}
{synopt:{opt lo:ng}}return nearest neighbors in long form{p_end}
{synopt:{opt near:count(#)}}find the {it:#} nearest neighbors{p_end}
{synopt:{opt i:gnoreself}}ignore the distance to self (when {it:baseid} == {it:nborid}){p_end}

{syntab:Wide form only}
{synopt:{opt g:enstub(str)}}prefix nearest neighbor variable names with {it:str}{p_end}

{syntab:Long form only}
{synopt:{opt wit:hin(#)}}return neighbors within a distance of {it:#} from each {it:baseid}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt li:mit(#)}}return no more than {it:#} nearest neighbors for each {it:baseid}{p_end}

{syntab:Distances}
{synopt:{opt mi:les}}return distances in miles{p_end}
{synopt:{opt ra:dius(#)}}custom radius {it:#} (in km) for spherical distances{p_end}
{synopt:{opt e:llipsoid}}use ellipsoidal distances{p_end}
{synopt:{opt a(#)}}custom ellipsoid; semi-major axis parameter {it:#}{p_end}
{synopt:{opt f(#)}}custom ellipsoid; reciprocal of the flattening parameter {it:#}{p_end}

{syntab:Compatibility}
{synopt:{opt o:ps(#)}}accepted for {cmd:geonear}; has no effect{p_end}
{synopt:{opt re:port(#)}}accepted for {cmd:geonear}; has no effect{p_end}
{synopt:{opt ma:xbases(str)}}accepted for {cmd:geonear}; has no effect{p_end}

{syntab:ctools}
{synopt:{opt threads(#)}}maximum number of threads{p_end}
{synopt:{opt verbose}}report the time spent in each step{p_end}
{synoptline}

{synopthdr:distance_options}
{synoptline}
{syntab:Main}
{synopt:{opt mi:les}}report distances in miles{p_end}

{syntab:Ellipsoid}
{synopt:{opt e:llipsoid(#1,#2)}}custom ellipsoid parameters ({it:a}, {it:f}^-1){p_end}

{syntab:Sphere}
{synopt:{opt s:phere}}calculate great-circle distances{p_end}
{synopt:{opt r:adius(#)}}custom radius {it:#} (in km){p_end}

{syntab:ctools}
{synopt:{opt threads(#)}}maximum number of threads{p_end}
{synopt:{opt verbose}}report the run time{p_end}
{synoptline}
{p2colreset}{...}

{marker description}{...}

{title:Description}

{* ctools-credit:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{pstd}
{cmd:cgeonear} reimplements {cmd:geonear} 2.0.3 (September 4, 2019) by Robert
Picard and {cmd:geodist} 1.1.0 (June 18, 2019) by Robert Picard. See
{help cgeonear##acknowledgments:Acknowledgments} for details.
{* ctools-credit:end}{...}

{pstd}{cmd:cgeonear} has two forms. Given a {cmd:using} file, it finds the
nearest neighbors of each location, as {cmd:geonear} does. Given four
coordinates, it calculates the distance between two points in each
observation, or between two given points, as {cmd:geodist} does. The two
forms take the options of the command they replace.

{pstd}Coordinates are in signed decimal degrees, positive for north and east
and negative for south and west. Latitudes range from -90 to 90 and
longitudes from -180 to 180. Distances are geodetic: the length of the
shortest curve between two points along the surface of a model of the earth,
a sphere (great-circle distances, haversine formula) or a reference ellipsoid
(Vincenty's (1975) formula, which may fail to find a solution for nearly
antipodal points). The nearest-neighbor form measures on the sphere unless
{opt ellipsoid} is specified; the distance form measures on the WGS 1984
ellipsoid unless {opt sphere} is specified, as in {cmd:geonear} and
{cmd:geodist}.

{pstd}{ul:Nearest neighbors.} For each location identified by {it:baseid} and
with coordinates {it:baselat} {it:baselon}, {cmd:cgeonear} finds in
{it:nborfile}, a Stata dataset, the nearest neighbor(s), identified by
{it:nborid} and located at {it:nborlat} {it:nborlon}. Distances are in
kilometers, and the distance variables are prefixed with {cmd:km_to_},
unless {opt miles} is specified (the prefix is then {cmd:mi_to_}).

{pstd}{cmd:geonear} splits the base locations into regions and compares each
region with the neighbors that may be near it. {cmd:cgeonear} builds a k-d
tree of the neighbor locations in three dimensions and searches it for every
base location in parallel. Straight-line distances bound geodetic ones from
below, so the search can skip every part of the tree that is too far away and
computes the geodetic distance only to the few neighbors that remain. The
neighbors found are the ones a comparison of every base with every neighbor
gives, which are also {cmd:geonear}'s.

{pstd}Each identifier is one location. Observations with a missing identifier,
latitude or longitude are ignored, the coordinates of an identifier must be
the same in all of its other observations, and {it:baseid} and {it:nborid} may
be numeric or string.

{pstd}In wide form (the default), {cmd:cgeonear} adds two variables for each
neighbor requested to the data in memory: the neighbor's identifier and its
distance from {it:baseid}. Every observation of a base location gets its
neighbors, including observations of that identifier with missing
coordinates; observations with a missing identifier or none of its
coordinates get missing values. The data are then ordered by {it:baseid}.

{pstd}In long form ({opt long}), the data in memory are replaced by one
observation per base location and neighbor found, with the variables
{it:baseid}, {it:nborid} and {cmd:km_to_}{it:nborid}, sorted by {it:baseid},
distance and {it:nborid}. {it:baseid} and {it:nborid} must then have different
names.

{pstd}{ul:Distances.} Each of {it:lat1 lon1 lat2 lon2} is a numeric variable, a
numeric scalar or a number. If any is a variable, {cmd:cgeonear} calculates
one distance for each observation in the sample, in parallel, and stores them
in {it:newvar}; otherwise it displays the distance and returns it in
{cmd:r(distance)}. To calculate the distances between every pair of points of
two datasets, or to find the nearest ones, use the nearest-neighbor form.

{marker options_near}{...}

{title:Options for nearest neighbors}

{dlgtab:Main}

{phang}{opt wide} returns the results in wide form. With {opt nearcount()}
omitted, the nearest neighbor is identified by the variables {cmd:nid} and
{cmd:km_to_nid}. Otherwise the variables are {cmd:nid1} {cmd:km_to_nid1}
{cmd:nid2} {cmd:km_to_nid2} ... {cmd:nid}{it:#} {cmd:km_to_nid}{it:#}, nearest
first. {opt genstub()} replaces {cmd:nid}. The identifier variables have the
storage type of {it:nborid}.

{phang}{opt long} returns the results in long form, one observation per
neighbor found. {opt within()} and {opt nearcount()} can be combined: a
neighbor that satisfies either is returned.

{phang}{opt nearcount(#)} requests the {it:#} nearest neighbors of each base
location; the default is 1. Neighbors at the same distance are ranked by
ascending {it:nborid}. In long form, {cmd:nearcount(0)} with {opt within()}
returns only the neighbors within that distance; base locations without any
are left out. In long form, neighbors at a distance of exactly 0 (the same
location) are always returned, as in {cmd:geonear}, whose {opt within()}
distance is 0 when not specified.

{phang}{opt ignoreself} ignores the distance from a base location to the
neighbor with the same identifier, for when {it:baseid} and {it:nborid}
identify the same set of locations; the nearest neighbor is then another
location. A string identifier cannot be compared with a numeric one
(error 109).

{dlgtab:Wide form only}

{phang}{opt genstub(str)} names the neighbor variables {it:str} and
{cmd:km_to_}{it:str} (or {it:str}{it:#} and {cmd:km_to_}{it:str}{it:#}) in
place of {cmd:nid}. The variables must not exist (error 110).

{dlgtab:Long form only}

{phang}{opt within(#)} returns every neighbor within a distance {it:#} of each
base location, in miles if {opt miles} is specified.

{phang}{opt limit(#)} keeps at most {it:#} neighbors for each base location,
the nearest, after {opt within()} and {opt nearcount()} have selected them. It
must not be less than {opt nearcount()}.

{dlgtab:Distances}

{phang}{opt miles} reports distances in miles (1.609344 km) and reads
{opt within()} in miles.

{phang}{opt radius(#)} is the radius of the sphere in km; the default is
6,371 km, the earth's mean radius.

{phang}{opt ellipsoid} measures distances on the WGS 1984 reference ellipsoid
(semi-major axis 6,378,137 m, flattening 1/298.257223563). Distances for
which Vincenty's iteration does not converge are missing.

{phang}{opt a(#)} and {opt f(#)} specify another reference ellipsoid: the
semi-major axis in meters and the reciprocal of the flattening, for example
{cmd:a(6377397.155) f(299.1528128)} for Bessel 1841. They imply
{opt ellipsoid} and must be given together.

{dlgtab:Compatibility}

{phang}{opt ops(#)}, {opt report(#)} and {opt maxbases()} set the size of
{cmd:geonear}'s regions and how often it reports progress. They are accepted
with {cmd:geonear}'s checks and have no effect.

{dlgtab:ctools}

{phang}{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
Omitting it or specifying 0 uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.

{phang}{opt verbose} reports the time spent preparing, reading the base and
neighbor locations, building the tree, searching it and writing the results.

{marker options_dist}{...}

{title:Options for distances}

{dlgtab:Main}

{phang}{opt generate(newvar)} names the new variable of distances, required
when a coordinate is a variable. Observations outside the sample, or with a
missing coordinate, get missing values; distances for which Vincenty's
iteration does not converge are {cmd:.a}, and their number is reported.

{phang}{opt miles} reports distances in miles; otherwise they are in
kilometers.

{dlgtab:Ellipsoid}

{phang}{opt ellipsoid(#1,#2)} specifies another reference ellipsoid: {it:#1}
is the semi-major axis (equatorial radius) in meters and {it:#2} the reciprocal
of the flattening; for example {cmd:ellipsoid(6377563.396,299.3249646)} for
Airy 1830.

{dlgtab:Sphere}

{phang}{opt sphere} requests great-circle distances.

{phang}{opt radius(#)} calculates great-circle distances on a sphere with a
radius of {it:#} km; the default is 6371, the earth's mean radius.

{dlgtab:ctools}

{phang}{opt threads(#)} sets the maximum number of threads, from 1 to 256.
Omitting it or specifying 0 uses the {help ctools##threads:ctools setting}.

{phang}{opt verbose} reports the run time.

{marker remarks}{...}

{title:Remarks}

{pstd}After a nearest-neighbor search, {cmd:cgeonear} shows the number of
unique base and neighbor locations, the threads used, the number of geodetic
distances computed and the run time.

{pstd}The distances are those {cmd:geonear} and {cmd:geodist} compute, to the
last digit: the haversine and Vincenty formulas follow their Stata
expressions operation by operation, and on Windows the plugin calls the
trigonometric functions Stata itself uses. The one exception is Stata's
{cmd:atan2()}, which Vincenty's formula calls and which differs from the
runtime's in the last bit for a few arguments in 100,000; an ellipsoid
distance can then differ in its last digit or two. Results are the same at
any number of threads.

{pstd}The distance form follows {cmd:geodist} 1.1.0: its checks, messages,
results and stored results. As in {cmd:geodist}, the range of a variable is
checked on the observations that are still in the sample once the variables
before it are taken into account, and the undocumented {opt maxiter(#)}
option (default 25) bounds Vincenty's iteration.

{pstd}Where the nearest-neighbor form differs from {cmd:geonear} 2.0.3:

{phang2}o In wide form, an existing variable with the name of a result
variable is an error (110); {cmd:geonear} leaves its values in place and adds
the others. A variable named {cmd:_merge} is allowed.{p_end}
{phang2}o In wide form, observations with the same {it:baseid} keep their
order. Data already in {it:baseid} order are left as they are; others are
reordered and marked as sorted by {it:baseid}. {cmd:geonear} orders them by
{it:baseid} with ties in no particular order and leaves no sort
marker.{p_end}
{phang2}o Neighbors at the same distance are always ranked by {it:nborid}.
In wide form with {opt nearcount()} above 1 or {opt ignoreself},
{cmd:geonear} can rank equidistant neighbors at different locations by their
distance from the middle of its region instead.{p_end}
{phang2}o With {opt ellipsoid} in wide form, {cmd:geonear} measures some
pairs from the neighbor to the base; Vincenty's formula is not symmetric in
the last digits, so such distances can differ from {cmd:cgeonear}'s by up to
about 2e-13 relative.{p_end}
{phang2}o A neighbor whose Vincenty distance does not converge ranks after all
others; {cmd:geonear} can then return the same neighbor more than once in
wide form.{p_end}
{phang2}o {cmd:strL} identifiers are not supported (error 109), and
{opt f()} must exceed 1.{p_end}
{phang2}o {cmd:r()} is cleared; {cmd:geonear} leaves the results of the last
command it ran internally.{p_end}

{pstd}As in {cmd:geonear}, the long-form result keeps the data label, the
characteristics and notes of the base data, everything that describes
{it:baseid} and {it:nborid}, and no other value labels (those of the location
variables, and labels no variable uses, are dropped); the notes of {it:nborfile} are appended to those of the data unless
the same note is already there, and its other dataset characteristics are
added where the data have none of that name. In wide form, only the dataset
notes and characteristics of {it:nborfile} are added.

{pstd}Neither {cmd:geonear} nor {cmd:geodist} is required.

{marker examples}{...}

{title:Examples}

{pstd}Base locations: 3,278 census block group centroids in Colorado; neighbor
locations: 1,000 cell towers.{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. set seed 123456}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. set obs 3278}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen bgid = _n}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen double bglat = 37 + (41 - 37) * runiform()}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen double bglon = -109 + (109 - 102) * runiform()}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. tempfile bg}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. save "`bg'"}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. set obs 1000}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen ctid = _n}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen double ctlat = 37 + (41 - 37) * runiform()}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. gen double ctlon = -109 + (109 - 102) * runiform()}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. tempfile cell}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. save "`cell'"}{p_end}

{pstd}The nearest other tower of each cell tower:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear ctid ctlat ctlon using "`cell'", n(ctid ctlat ctlon) ignoreself}{p_end}

{pstd}All towers within 50 km of each tower:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. use "`cell'", clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. rename ctid ctid0}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear ctid0 ctlat ctlon using "`cell'", n(ctid ctlat ctlon) ignoreself long within(50) near(0)}{p_end}

{pstd}The nearest tower and every tower within 5 km of each block group:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. use "`bg'", clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear bgid bglat bglon using "`cell'", n(ctid ctlat ctlon) within(5) long}{p_end}

{pstd}The three nearest block groups of each tower, in wide form, on the
ellipsoid:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. use "`cell'", clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear ctid ctlat ctlon using "`bg'", n(bgid bglat bglon) near(3) genstub(bgid) ellipsoid}{p_end}

{pstd}The distance in miles from the Michigan Stadium to the North Terminal of
the Detroit Metro Airport, on the ellipsoid and on a sphere:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear 42.265837 -83.748696 42.207667 -83.356022, miles}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear 42.265837 -83.748696 42.207667 -83.356022, miles sphere}{p_end}

{pstd}The distance in kilometers from each tower to the stadium:{p_end}

{phang2}{cmd:. use "`cell'", clear}{p_end}
{phang2}{cmd:. cgeonear 42.265837 -83.748696 ctlat ctlon, gen(d)}{p_end}

{marker results}{...}

{title:Stored results}

{pstd}The distance form with no coordinate in a variable stores the following
in {cmd:r()}:

{synoptset 18 tabbed}{...}
{p2col 5 18 22 2: Scalars}{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(distance)}}the distance{p_end}
{synopt:{cmd:r(iterations)}}the number of iterations (ellipsoid only){p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}The nearest-neighbor form and the distance form with variables store
nothing.


{* ctools-attribution:begin (generated by scripts/sync_attribution.py; edit config/attribution.json)}{...}
{marker acknowledgments}{...}
{title:Acknowledgments}

{pstd}
{cmd:cgeonear} reimplements two commands written by Robert Picard. Given a
using file it follows {cmd:geonear}, and given four coordinates it follows
{cmd:geodist}: their syntax, options, distance formulas, messages, and
results.

{pstd}
{cmd:cgeonear} is pinned to these versions:

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:geonear}{p_end}
{p2col:Authors}Robert Picard{p_end}
{p2col:Version}2.0.3{p_end}
{p2col:Date}September 4, 2019{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! version 2.0.3  04sep2019 Robert Picard, robertpicard@gmail.com}{p_end}
{p2col:Source}SSC ({cmd:ssc install geonear}), distributed September 14,
2019.{p_end}
{p2col:Latest version?}Yes, as of November 25, 2026.{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{p2colset 9 27 29 2}{...}
{p2col:Command}{cmd:geodist}{p_end}
{p2col:Authors}Robert Picard{p_end}
{p2col:Version}1.1.0{p_end}
{p2col:Date}June 18, 2019{p_end}
{p2col:Header line}{cmd:*! version 1.1.0  18jun2019 Robert Picard, picard@netbox.com}{p_end}
{p2col:Source}SSC ({cmd:ssc install geodist}), distributed June 24,
2019.{p_end}
{p2col:Latest version?}Yes, as of November 25, 2026.{p_end}
{p2colreset}{...}

{pstd}
{cmd:cgeonear} changes only when ctools is updated to newer versions of these
commands and tested against them. {cmd:which} shows the version of each
command installed on your machine. {help ctools##acknowledgments:help ctools}
lists the pinned versions of all ctools commands.

{pstd}
Please report bugs and questions about {cmd:cgeonear} at
{browse "https://github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues":github.com/mdroste/stata-ctools/issues}.
The authors of {cmd:geonear} and {cmd:geodist} do not maintain {cmd:cgeonear}.
Undocumented differences between {cmd:cgeonear} and the original commands are
bugs in {cmd:cgeonear}.

{pstd}
If you use {cmd:cgeonear} in published work, please cite the original command:

